<!--
  The breaking-change inventory for this release. Generated, never hand-written
  (dotnet pack -p:ApiCompatGenerateSuppressionFile=true), which also means a regeneration drops this
  comment and the CRLF it writes needs normalising back to LF. Six entries, all deliberate:

  CP0002 x1 WithGuards(int, int, int, int): the all-int overload went when the four guards became
  int?, so naming one stopped silently resetting the other three to the engine constants.

  CP0014 x2 PaginateComposedQuery<TEntity>.SortBy: the property and its getter lost their nullable
  annotation when a tie-breaker became mandatory at Build().

  CP0014 x2 PaginateLinkContext.Equals / GetHashCode: synthesized by the record, now hand-written,
  so they no longer carry CompilerGeneratedAttribute.

  CP0014 x1 PaginateLikeDefaults.Strategy.set: the auto-property setter gained a body that rejects
  null at the assignment instead of deferring a NullReferenceException into query composition.

  Every CP0014 here is a member whose BEHAVIOUR changed on purpose; losing the compiler-generated
  marker is a side effect of writing it out. They are visible only because the ApiCompat attribute
  rule is now on — annotations and those markers live in metadata the signature comparison does not
  read, and PublicAPI.Shipped.txt records a member only as line text, so each of these went in with
  no diagnostic from anything before the rule was enabled.

  Delete this file when PackageValidationBaselineVersion moves past 10.0.3: every entry exists only
  against that baseline, and a stale suppression hides the next accidental break behind the same target.
-->
